  "worked_solution": "First, we move the constant term to the right-hand side, the $2n$ term to the left-hand side, and complete the square on the left-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}^{2}-11 & =2n \\\\ {n}^{2}-2n & =11 \\\\ {n}^{2}-2\\cdot 1\\cdot n & =11 \\\\ {n}^{2}-2\\cdot 1\\cdot n+{1}^{2}-{1}^{2} & =11 \\\\ (n-1{)}^{2}-{1}^{2} & =11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we isolate the perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(n-1{)}^{2}-{1}^{2} & =11 \\\\ (n-1{)}^{2} & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $p=-1,q=12,$ and $p+q=11.$\n\nNext, we solve the equation using the square-root method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(n-1{)}^{2} & =12 \\\\ \\sqrt{(n-1{)}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{12} \\\\ |n-1| & =\\sqrt{12} \\\\ n-1 & =\\pm 2\\sqrt{3} \\\\ n & =1\\pm 2\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
